      "hit": null,
      "abstained": false,
      "answer": "Objective self-awareness is the ability of the patient to recognize personal identity and relate that to the examiner, as well as to understand language by following instructions to perform actions. \n\nThis concept is rooted in the neurological examination, where self-awareness is assessed through specific subtests. One such test involves the patient's ability to understand language by following complex instructions, such as “touch your right finger to your left elbow and then to your right knee.” Another aspect of objective self-awareness is the patient’s awareness of their immediate circumstances, including time, place, and personal identity. This awareness is crucial for determining the patient’s orientation, which is a fundamental component of cognitive function. The material discusses orientation as the patient’s awareness of their surroundings, including the date, their location, and their sense of self. [p.664]"
